![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

There is a logic bug where enemies are removed from the `enemies` array before their `reachedEnd` or death status is processed for rewards and base damage in the main game loop. Moving this logic into the `WaveManager.update` method ensures that side effects are handled exactly when an enemy is removed.

```javascript
    update(deltaTime, scene, gameState) {
        if (this.currentWave < 0) return;

        const waveConfig = CONFIG.waveConfigs[this.currentWave];

        // Spawn enemies
        if (this.spawnIndex < waveConfig.count) {
            this.spawnTimer -= deltaTime;
            if (this.spawnTimer <= 0) {
                const enemyType = waveConfig.enemyType === 'mixed'
                    ? (Math.random() > 0.6 ? 'fast' : 'strong')
                    : waveConfig.enemyType;
                const enemy = new Enemy(this.pathWaypoints, enemyType);
                this.enemies.push(enemy);
                scene.add(enemy.mesh);
                this.spawnIndex++;
                this.spawnTimer = waveConfig.delay;
            }
        }

        // Update enemies
        for (let i = this.enemies.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            const enemy = this.enemies[i];
            enemy.update(deltaTime);
            if (!enemy.isAlive) {
                if (enemy.reachedEnd) {
                    gameState.damageBase(1);
                } else if (!enemy.rewardGiven) {
                    gameState.earnMoney(enemy.reward);
                    enemy.rewardGiven = true;
                }
                scene.remove(enemy.mesh);
                this.enemies.splice(i, 1);
            }
        }
    }
```

![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

The main update loop contains redundant and broken logic for rewards and base damage (as enemies are removed before these checks run). Consolidating this logic into the `WaveManager.update` call simplifies the loop and fixes the bug.

```javascript
    update(deltaTime) {
        if (!this.isRunning) return;

        // Update wave and handle rewards/damage
        this.waveManager.update(deltaTime, this.scene.scene, this.gameState);

        // Update towers
        for (let tower of this.scene.towers) {
            tower.update(deltaTime, this.waveManager.getActiveEnemies(), this.scene.projectiles, this.scene.scene);
        }

        // Update projectiles
        updateProjectiles(this.scene.projectiles, this.scene.scene, deltaTime);

        // Check for wave completion
        if (this.gameState.currentWaveActive && this.waveManager.isWaveComplete()) {
            this.gameState.currentWaveActive = false;
            this.gameState.nextWave();

            if (!this.gameState.isGameOver) {
                setTimeout(() => this.startNextWave(), 1000);
            }
        }

        // Update UI
        this.ui.updateStats(this.gameState.money, this.gameState.lives, this.gameState.waveIndex);

        // Check game over
        if (this.gameState.isGameOver) {
            this.isRunning = false;
            this.ui.showGameOver(this.gameState.isWon);
        }
    }
```

![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

The `rewardGiven` property is used in the game loop to prevent multiple rewards for the same enemy, but it is not initialized in the constructor. Initializing it here ensures a consistent object shape and avoids potential issues with undefined checks.

```suggestion
        this.slowEndTime = 0;
        this.rewardGiven = false;
    }
```

![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

This line is inefficient because it creates a new object literal `{ x: this.x, z: this.z }` and calls `Math.sqrt` (via `distance`) for every enemy, every frame, for every tower. Using squared distance comparison avoids both object allocation and the expensive square root operation.

```javascript
            const dx = this.x - e.position.x;
            const dz = this.z - e.position.z;
            return (dx * dx + dz * dz) <= (this.config.range * this.config.range);
```

![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

Creating a new `PlaneGeometry` and `Mesh` on every click is inefficient and will cause frequent garbage collection. It is better to create a single raycasting plane once and reuse it.

```javascript
        if (!this.raycastPlane) {
            this.raycastPlane = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.PlaneGeometry(CONFIG.mapWidth + 10, CONFIG.mapHeight + 10));
            this.raycastPlane.rotation.x = -Math.PI / 2;
            this.raycastPlane.visible = false;
            this.scene.add(this.raycastPlane);
        }

        const intersects = raycaster.intersectObject(this.raycastPlane);
```
